  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why did Rome have two consuls?",
      "a": "So that no single person held all the power. Each consul could block the other, and they served only one year, after Rome threw out its last king in 509 BCE.",
      "asks": [
        "What did consuls do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What did the Roman Senate do?",
      "a": "The Senate was a council of experienced men, mostly from wealthy families. It advised the consuls, managed money and dealt with foreign countries. Its advice was rarely ignored.",
      "asks": [
        "Who was in the Roman Senate?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a veto?",
      "a": "Veto is Latin for I forbid. A Roman tribune could use it to block an action or law that harmed ordinary citizens. Many leaders and councils have a veto today.",
      "asks": [
        "What does veto mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who could vote in the Roman Republic?",
      "a": "Adult male citizens could vote in the assemblies. Women, enslaved people and most foreigners could not, and in some assemblies the votes of the rich counted for more.",
      "asks": [
        "Could everyone vote in Rome?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How did the Roman Republic end?",
      "a": "After years of civil wars between powerful generals, like Julius Caesar, Caesar's adopted heir Octavian took control. In 27 BCE he became Augustus, the first emperor.",
      "asks": [
        "Why did the Republic become an empire?"
      ]
    }
  ],
